@@ -64,6 +64,56 @@ Orca launches every supported agent with its full-autonomy permission flag pre-a
 
 If you want different defaults for an agent, open **Settings → Agents**, expand the agent's row, and edit its **launch arguments** — Orca remembers the override and applies it on every subsequent launch. A **Reset** button next to the field puts the shipped flag back if you want to revert.
 
+## Ojak-managed omp launches
+
+When the local omp configuration contains a verified Ojak-owned accounts extension, Orca
+checks Ojak admission **before creating the terminal or structured agent process**. Both
+omp ACP chats and Pi RPC launches configured to use omp share the terminal launch queue.
+Other agents, unmanaged omp configurations, SSH execution, WSL, and reattaching an existing
+process do not use the client's Ojak gate.
+
+This source fork is version `1.5.0-ojak.1` and supports the coupled omp
+`18.12.0-ojak.1` ACP client explicitly. Other prerelease omp builds remain unsupported;
+the existing stable-release compatibility rule is unchanged.
+
+During resource pressure or a temporary Ojak service outage, the pane shows a waiting notice.
+Terminal launch selections and prompts remain in Orca's existing host journal; structured
+messages remain in their conversation journal and durable draft queue. Credentials, child
+environments, and executable command lines are not saved in the admission queue. **Cancel**
+stops an unsent launch. **Copy saved input** copies available launch text; **Dismiss** removes
+a cancelled or failed entry. **Other saved Ojak launches** exposes entries outside this pane.
+
+Capacity returning resumes waiting launches automatically, one local startup at a time, with
+at least two seconds between startups. Readiness is a sample, not a reservation: Ojak still
+owns actual model admission. A 503 admission-control response waits only when it explicitly
+proves rejection before submission. Authentication, other HTTP errors, invalid responses, and
+journal failures stop the launch visibly instead of being treated as resource pressure. The queue holds at most
+32 saved launches and 4 MiB of serialized intent. A full queue refuses additional launches;
+keep the original draft and cancel/dismiss an unsent entry before trying again.
+
+After shutdown or a crash, Orca automatically reconstructs saved **queued, unsent** launches
+whose original local workspace and pane still exist. It re-reads current commands, permissions,
+environment and credentials rather than executing saved shell text. Structured recovery resumes
+the existing durable message queue; it does not append a duplicate user message or a continuation
+prompt. A normal shutdown suspends waits without cancelling their accepted intent; an explicit
+Cancel remains cancelled after reopening.
+
+A first structured launch may have a saved workspace tab before its provider-visible index
+exists. Recovery checks that persisted tab and reuses the reserved session and tab identities
+when no provider conversation was created. It does not require another Create or Send.
+The waiting notice sits above the chat so the original input draft remains accessible.
+
+Closed or missing owners are never recreated automatically. Select a valid local workspace and
+use **Resume in this workspace** for an orphaned terminal launch. A saved structured chat resumes
+in its original workspace, preserving its conversation identity; a deleted workspace must be
+restored before that chat can resume. Older saved entries without recovery selections remain
+available for copying and cancellation, not command replay.
+
+A launch marked **submitted** is never automatically replayed, including a recovered terminal
+whose process started but whose initial prompt delivery is unconfirmed. Existing terminal
+ownership and structured-session recovery still decide whether a process is live, unverifiable,
+or exited; admission never overrides that decision.
+
 ## Restart chip
 
 When an agent exits (clean or crash), the tab shows a **Restart** chip. One click rehydrates the same agent with the same working directory. Codex's restart chip also preserves the current account (see [Hot-swap Codex accounts](/docs/agents/codex-hot-swap)).
